Keywords
Micro-porous materials | Ullmann coupling reaction | Copper-catalyzed | Rich N-sites | $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ separation | $\mathsf C_{2}\mathsf H_{2}/\mathsf C\mathsf O_{2}$ separation
## Comprehensive Summary
Acetylene purification from methane is challenging in the field of porous organic polymers (POPs). Herein, we have provided one-pot Ullmann coupling reaction to synthesize a series of POPs with rich N-sites, named FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ , wherein the low-cost and non-toxic ${\mathsf{C u}}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{2}$ and environmental-friendly glycerol are employed as catalyst and solvent, respectively. These POPs constructed by aromatic amine and haloarene monomers exhibit different steric configurations and pore sizes. Among these, FJU-POP-4 with abundant N-functional sites and suitable pore size exhibits ultra-high selective separation for $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ (307.1, 50 : 50) at ambient conditions, the highest among the reported MOFs and POPs with nitrogen-sites as only functional species. Dynamic fixed bed breakthrough and regeneration experiments confirm structural stability and reproducibility, indicating their promising application in selective separation.
## Background and Originality Content
Acetylene $(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2})$ as an important chemical raw material and high-energy gas, is widely used in the modern chemical industry and daily life. In industry, the main method of producing acetylene is cracking methane, inevitably leading to unreacted methane and side-product carbon dioxide. The $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ separation is of great significance, because the recovery of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ from $C H_{4}$ can improve the quality of natural gases while providing an additional source of $C_{2}H_{2}$ for chemical processing and conversion. The conventional method used to separate $\mathsf C_{2}/\mathsf C_{1}$ mixtures is the low temperature vacuum distillation, which is costly and energy-consumption. Crystalline porous materials, particularly metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), have significant advantages in high efficient and energy-saving $\mathsf C_{2}/\mathsf C_{1}$ selective separation.[1-2] Immobilization of functional sites, such as open metal sites and Lewis basic sites, on the pore surfaces, as well as adjustment of pore size for a sieving effect have been used to enhance the adsorption selectivity of $C_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ in MOFs.[3-8] But the requirement of long-term stability does limit the practical application of these materials under industrially relevant conditions.
Porous organic polymers (POPs) have rapidly been emerging as very promising adsorbents because of their high physicochemical stability, large specific surface area, low skeleton density and various functionalities, which have been widely applied in industrial sewage treatment,[9] gas storage,[10-11] gas separation,[12-13] photocatalysis,[14] supercapacitors,[15-16] hydrogen evolution[17] and lymphatic targeting imaging.[18] Due to the stronger Lewis acidity of $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ compared with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $C H_{4},$ [19-20] nitrogen-rich POPs containing N-heterocycles, aromatic amines or alkylamines could afford stronger interactions with ${\mathsf C}_{2}{\mathsf H}_{2}$ molecules, which dramatically enhanced $C_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ adsorption. However, the issue of $C H_{4}$ co-adsorption in these materials is serious, caused by the low-density of binding sites within the networks as well as the difficulty in precisely tuning the pore sizes on a $3{\-}6\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ scale, relative to the kinetic diameter of hydrocarbon molecules. To date, three reported POPs built by N-functionalization showed effective $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ separation.[21-22] A series of POPs with rich $C-N$ are synthesized using DPTA as the building unit, which show effective separation performance for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C H}}_{4}$ .[23] In addition, many synthetic approaches have been taken to promote the development of POPs in the past decade, such as Suzuki,[24-28] Friedel-Crafts alkylation reactions,[29-34] cyclotrimerization[35-38] Sonogashira coupling,[39-43] Buchwald-Hartwig (BH coupling),[44-45] Yamamoto,[46-47] and Diazo-Coupling reaction (Table S1).[48-49] However, costly and toxic catalysts (e.g., catalysts containing Pd or Ni) or polluting solvents (e.g., DMF, THF, DMSO, ${\mathsf{C H}}_{2}{\mathsf{C l}}_{2}$ and $N a N O_{2})$ are required, which significantly limit the further development of POPs.[50]
It has been proved that organic small molecules with C—N bonds can be achieved by Ullmann coupling reaction through copper-catalyzed amines and aryl halides.[51] Inspired by this reaction, we employed Ullmann coupling into the synthesis of POPs by cross-coupling aryl polyamine with polyhaloarene, wherein low-cost and non-toxic ${\mathsf{C u}}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{2}$ is used as catalyst and environmental-friendly glycerol is solvent. It provides a green and economical method for stable conjugated POPs with $C-N$ bonds. More importantly, the POP networks can be systematically modified by the steric configurations and functional sites of the monomers. Since the POP networks can be systematically modified by the steric configurations and functional sites of the monomers, various aryl polyamine and polyhaloarene with different steric configurations are selected. Here, tetrahedral TAPM is chosen to react with triangular TBPA, tetrahedral TBPM and rod-like DBBZ, respectively, through onepot condensation.
Meanwhile, in order to better compare the effects of copper catalyst and palladium catalyst on the reaction system, PLDA and PBPA are also used as building units. As a result, a series of novel microporous polymers with excellent stability have been synthesized (Scheme 1), named FJU-POP-n. Among them, FJU-POP-4 performs the highest selective separation for $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ (50 : 50) at $298~\mathsf{K}$ at $100~\mathsf{k P a}$ , attributed to the abundant N-functional sites and suitable pore size. And its IAST selectivity up to 307.1 is the highest among the reported MOFs and POPs with N-sites as only functional species. Meanwhile, FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ show efficient separation of $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ in dynamic fixed bed breakthrough experiments and regeneration experiments, revealing that these microporous polymers could be a promising candidate for gas storage and separation.
## Results and Discussion
Using ${\mathsf{C u}}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{2}$ catalyst and environmental-friendly glycerol as solvent, a series novel FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ is obtained via Ullmann coupling reaction. The successful synthesis of FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ from the monomers was confirmed by FTIR (Figure S10), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (Figure S10) and solid $^{13}{\mathsf C}$ CP/MAS NMR spectra (Figure S9).
As shown in the FT-IR spectra, the bands around $3379~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ and $3180~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ can be attributed to $-N H_{2}$ stretching vibrations and $1624~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ to $-N H_{2}$ deformation, respectively, confirming the existence of primary amine group in TBPA and PLDA (Figure S11). And the peaks around $1075c m^{-1}$ for aromatic $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{B}\mathsf{r}$ groups, which can be observed in TBPA, TBPM and DBBZ (Figure S11), disappear in the spectra of the FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ , further approving the successful cross-coupling of aryl polyamine with polyhaloarene. And the distinct bands corresponding to the ‒NH‒ $(3404~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1})$ and $-\mathsf{C}-\mathsf{N}-(1289\mathsf{c m}^{-1})$ are present in the spectra of FJU-POP- $\boldsymbol{n}$ . Besides, various types of carbon signals can be drawn from the $^{13}{\mathsf C}$ MAS NMR. The NMR spectra of the polymers display five main resonances at $\delta$ about 63.0, 145.0, 135.0, 128.0 and 115.0, respectively. The peaks within $\delta\ 114.7-145.0$ are attributed to carbons in benzene in FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ , while the peak at $\delta63.0$ is attributed to central carbons in the tetraphenyl methane of FJU-POP-1, 2 and 3. According to the results of FT-IR and $^{13}{\mathsf C}$ MAS NMR, these compounds of FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ have been successfully synthesized by Ullmann coupling reaction. We discovered from the 13C MAS NMR spectrum that the four compounds display aliphatic carbon signals at $\delta$ about 28.8. Therefore, it could be speculated that there was a small amount of catalyst residue in the compounds. To verify this speculation and to investigate whether the reaction was completed or not, XPS was used to characterize the Cu oxidation state and Br. As shown in the XPS spectra (Figures S10, S12 and S13), the coexistence of $\mathtt{C u2p3/2}$ is evidenced by a shoulder observed on the main peak at $933.6\ \mathsf{e V},$ which is assigned to $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I I})$ species, with ${\sim}1.0\%$ Cu detected in the sample. The Br signal was found at $70.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ in FJU-POP-1, FJU-POP-2, and FJU-POP-3 with the contents of around $0.12\%$ , $0.78\%$ , and $0.18\%$ ， respectively. However, it is not detected in FJU-POP-4. The existence of Cu suggested that there was a trace amount of catalyst in the polymers, which also explained the presence of a small amount of aliphatic carbon in $^{13}{\mathsf C}$ MAS NMR. At the same time, the trace amount of Br also reflected a complete reaction. By the XPS spectroscopy (Figure S10), we did not find any residual $\mathsf{K}^{+}$ or trapped solvent within the channel of POPs. During the post-synthesis treatments of FJU-POP-n, the samples were immersed and centrifuged in acetone and water, then were consecutively activated by ${\mathsf{C H}}_{2}{\mathsf{C l}}_{2}$ and acetone for $24\ h$ and dried at $80~^{\circ}C$ for another $24\ h$ . Presumably KOH and solvents have been removed from the channel.
The amorphous nature of FJU-POP- $n$ was also revealed by the broad diffraction peak at $2\vartheta=16.7^{\circ}$ in powder X-ray diffraction pattern (Figure S14), which implies an amorphous phase of FJU-POP-n. The morphologies of the polymers were observed to use both scanning electron microscopy (Figure S15) and high-resolution TEM (Figure S16). It reveals that these hypercrosslinked polymers are composed of loose aggregated particles with variable size and the irregular surface, which is in good agreement with self-assembly behavior for this kind of monomers.[52-53] It is noteworthy that the aggregated morphology of POPs differs from each other. FJU-POP-1 exists as mostly loosely aggregated spheres ranging within hundreds of nanometers. FJU-POP-2 and FJU-POP-4 are composed of particles of various sizes ranging from hundreds of nanometers to several micrometers. FJU-POP-3 can form a coral reef appearance by its uniform spherical nanoparticles on the surface of block particles. The TEM images of FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ show that their disordered porous layered structure is consistent with the PXRD results. It can be seen that the pore distribution of FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ is uniform with the width at around $0.7\ \mathsf{n m}$ . The thermal stabilities of FJU-POP-n were studied by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) under a nitrogen atmosphere (Figure S17). All of FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ have high decomposition temperatures, exceeding $300^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ which can be attributed to their cross-linked structures and aromatic polymer skeletons.
The adsorption of $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ by FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ at $77\upkappa$ was almost negligible and it may be due to the activated diffusion effects in liquid nitrogen atmosphere.[54-56] Therefore, the porosity parameters of FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ were investigated by physical sorption experiments of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ at ${\sf196}~\sf K.$ As demonstrated in Figure 1a and Figure S18, FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ showed similar isotherms, and their gas absorption sharply increased at lower relative pressure. Usually, the larger building block benefits an increase in the interpenetration of amorphous structures, thus increasing the specific surface area.[57] With the increasing of building block size, the BET surface area of FJU-POP-1 $(285.8\mathrm{~\overline{{~m}}^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}})$ was higher than that of FJU-POP-3 $(218.9\ \mathsf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1})$ and FJU-POP-4 $(204.2~\mathsf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1})$ . But the BET surface area of FJU-POP-2 $(265.3~\mathsf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1})$ with the largest building blocks was slightly smaller than that of FJU-POP-1. It is probably because more severe steric hindrance in FJU-POP-2 leads to incomplete coupling reaction, which inhibits molecular weight growth.[58] The overall pore volumes of FJU-POP-n were calculated to be 0.22, 0.20, 0.17 and $0.14~{\mathsf{c m}}^{3}{\cdot}{\mathsf{g}}^{-1}.$ , respectively. In addition, it is interesting that FJU-POP-4 showed higher BET surface area and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capacity when compared with microporous polymer PTPA-1 that was prepared via BH coupling of identical monomers with Pd as catalyst and anhydrous toluene as solvent.[44] As demonstrated in Figure S19 (Table S4), the BET surface area of FJU-POP-4 $(204.2\mathsf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1})$ was higher than that of PTPA-1 $(52\mathsf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1})$ . Meanwhile, the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake value is $5.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ for FJU-POP-4, whereas the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake is $3.7~\mathrm{wt\%}$ for PTPA-1 under 273 K, 1 bar. Unlike the highly active Pd catalysts in PTPA-1, $\mathsf{C u}(|)$ active species, converted from $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I I})$ via disproportionation reaction, is much milder in coupling reaction for FJU-POP-4, which may cause the conjugated segments to separate from each other and avoid pore closure. [59]
The pore size distribution calculated by the no-local density functional theory (NLDFT) showed that all of the frameworks have relatively narrow and uniform pore size distribution, which are in good agreement with the shape of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ isotherms. In general, the microporous size distribution of POPs is shifted to the larger pores when larger building blocks are used. FJU-POP-4 with the smallest building blocks exhibits micropores centered at $5.4\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and $8.4\mathring{\mathsf{A}},$ and FJU-POP-1 with larger building blocks shows micropores located at $5.8\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and $8.7\mathrm{\AA}.$ . It can be thus objective evidence of the presence of micropores in these polymer materials, and also confirms that the porosity can be changed by regulating the structural unit to improve the application performance of POPs.
The stable microporous structure and N-rich sites within the frameworks of FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ prompted us to examine their capacities for gas adsorption and separation. The adsorption isotherms of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2},$ ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $C H_{4}$ on FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ are measured at $273\mathsf{K}$ and $296\textsf{K}$ at 1 bar (Figure S20 and Figure 1), respectively. The $C_{2}H_{2}$ uptakes values are 33.7, 26.5, 21.5 and $27.0c m^{3}{\cdot}{\underline{{\mathbf{g}}}}^{-1}$ for FJU-POP-1, FJU-POP-2, FJU-POP-3 and FJU-POP-4, respectively, whereas the $C H_{4}$ uptakes are 6.2, 4.4, 3.1 and $3.6~\mathsf{c m}^{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ for FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ under $296\mathsf{K},$ 1 bar. The $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ uptakes are up to 7.5 times higher than the $C H_{4}$ uptakes at $296\ \mathsf{K}.$ In addition, the saturated adsorption value of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ is much higher than the storage capacity of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ at $298\textsf{K}$ Furthermore, it should be noted that the ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms of FJU-POP- $\boldsymbol{n}$ show a rapid uptake at low pressure, whereas the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $C H_{4}$ uptakes are much lower at this pressure, suggesting that FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ could be the promising material for selective separation toward $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ and $\mathsf C_{2}\mathsf H_{2}/\mathsf C\mathsf O_{2}$ mixtures. The ideal species adsorbed solution theory (IAST) was used to test the separation selectively of $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ (50/50, V/V) and $\mathsf C_{2}\mathsf H_{2}/\mathsf C\mathsf O_{2}(50/50,$ ， V/V) mixtures.[60] As shown in Figure S38, the measured experimental data on pure-component adsorption isotherms for ${\mathsf{C H}}_{4},$ ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ on FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ at 273 and $296~\mathsf{K}$ were successfully fitted by the Virial equation, respectively. The IAST selectivity of FJU-POP-4 for ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ over $C H_{4}$ $\mid_{4}(50/50,V/V)$ is calculated to be 307.1 at 1.0 bar and $296\mathsf{K},$ which is higher than that of FJU-POP-1 (85.1), FJU-POP-2 (102.8) and FJU-POP-3 (230.1) (Figure 2). It can be attributed the higher concentration of N-functional sites in the FJU-POP-4 frameworks. Moreover, it is the highest compared with those of the previously reported materials with N-rich sites as only functional species, such as Cu–TDPAT (127.1),[61] PMOF-3a (156.5),[62] JLU-MOF66 (86.2),[63] ZJU-198a (80.0),[64] TT-POP-1 (40.1),[22] TT-POP-2 $(48.4)^{[22]}$ and $P C B\cdot N H_{2}$ (21).[21] Similarly, the IAST selectivity of FJU-POP-4 for $\mathsf C_{2}\mathsf H_{2}/\mathsf C\mathsf O_{2}$ lies in the ranges from 10.3 to 4.0, which are quite high as well (Figure S39).[65]
The isosteric heat of adsorption $(Q_{\mathrm{st}})$ for FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ was calculated with the adsorption isotherms on the basis of the virial equation and derived from the $273\textsf{K}$ and $296~\mathsf{K}$ to evaluate the adsorption affinity. As shown in Figures S22—S33, the values of virial coefficients $A_{0}$ and $A_{1}$ are simulated by formulas S1 and S2, respectively. The values of the virial coefficients $A_{0}$ and $A_{1}$ were then used to calculate the isosteric heat of adsorption. At near zero uptake, the adsorption enthalpies of $C_{2}H_{2}$ in FJU-POP-1, FJU-POP-2, FJU-POP-3, and FJU-POP-4 are 22.1, 32.3, 38.0 and $32.1~{\mathsf{k J}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{m o l}}^{-1}$ (Figures S34—S36), respectively, which is comparable with the values of some porous materials, such as FJU-90a $(25.1\ \mathsf{k J}\cdot\mathsf{m o l}^{-1})^{[66]}$ and SNNU-65-Cu-Ga $(31.7~\mathsf{k J}\cdot\mathsf{m o l}^{-1})$ ).[67] The research results indicate the stronger affinity of FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ toward ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2},$ consistent with the drastically increased absorption of ${\mathsf C}_{2}{\mathsf H}_{2}$ in the low-pressure zone in Figure 2 and Figure S21. FJU-POP-n possesses abundant surface functional sites including $\mathsf{C-H,N{-H}}$ and $C-N$ . The electrostatic potential of $C-N$ functional group displays negative distribution ${\dot{(\delta)}},^{[68]}$ and the N sites interact with the H atoms of $C_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ . Meanwhile, the ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{H}}$ and ${\mathsf{N}}{-}{\mathsf{H}}$ sites of phenyl with positive distribution $(\delta^{+})$ interact with the $\pi$ electrons of gas molecules. As confirmed by the surface potential energy of $C_{2}H_{2}$ (Figure S37), $C_{2}H_{2}$ has a stronger interaction via ${\sf H-C\equiv C\mathrm{-}{\sf H\cdot\cdot\cdot N}}$ bonds and π-π interactions with FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ .
To further investigate the separations performance of $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ and $\mathsf C_{2}\mathsf H_{2}/\mathsf C\mathsf O_{2}$ mixtures, the lab-scale fixed-bed breakthrough experiments are conducted by the $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{H e}$ (5/5/90, $V/V/V$ samples and the $\mathsf C_{2}\mathsf H_{2}/\mathsf C\mathsf O_{2}$ (5/5/90, V/V/V) mixture gas under 1 bar and $296~\mathsf{K}$ (Figure S40). As shown in Figure 3a, highly efficient separation of $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ mixture can be implemented by FJU-POP-n: $C H_{4}$ through the fixed-bed was eluted firstly, while $C_{2}H_{2}$ breakthrough did not happen until $27\ \mathrm{min}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . It must be noted that the roll-up behavior only appeared for ${\mathsf{C H}}_{4},$ which can be owed to the partial substitution of adsorbed $C H_{4}$ on the adsorption sites by the stronger adsorptive $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ in the mixtures gas.[69] The dynamic uptake capacities of FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ for ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ were calculated by using the breakthrough curve, and were found to be 0.16, 0.15, 0.12 and $0.24\ {\mathrm{mmol}}/{\mathrm{g}},$ respectively. By the breakthrough curve, the separation factors (Table S4), $\alpha=(q_{1}/y_{2})/(y_{1}/q_{2}),$ for FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ were calculated to be 6.1, 6.5, 5.7 and 6.9, respectively, comparable to those of other reported MOF materials.[70-71] As shown in Figure S41 and Table S5, the breakthrough curve shows that ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ can be completely separated from ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . To ensure the recyclability and facile regeneration of FJU-POP-n, multiple dynamic breakthrough experiments are performed for FJU-POP-4. Regeneration experiments showed that FJU-POP-4 were steady during ten dynamic breakthrough tests (Figure 3b, Figure S42). All these results strongly verified that FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ possess structural stability and reproducibility, and that they are the promising material for selective separation of $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ and $\mathsf C_{2}\mathsf H_{2}/\mathsf C\mathsf O_{2}$ .
## Conclusions
In conclusion, we have prepared a series of microporous organic polymers FJU-POP- $\cdot n$ for highly selective $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ separation by Ullmann coupling reactions for the first time. Ullmann coupling reactions not only provide low-cost and green method for ${\mathsf{P O P s}},$ but also immobilize abundant N-functional sites on the pore surface of POPs without chemical post-treatment for the improved effective effect for gas separation. Due to the microporous and the enriched aromatic amino groups of these FJU-POP-n, they exhibit ultra-high selective separation for ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ . Dynamic breakthrough experiments demonstrate its outstanding separation performance to $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ and $\mathsf C_{2}\mathsf H_{2}/\mathsf C\mathsf O_{2}$ . It is believed that this work represents an outstanding example for method strategy of POPs. The Ullmann coupling reaction extends the application of basic organic chemical reactions in POPs and endows POPs with the greatest potential for application.
## Experimental
## Synthesis of FJU-POP-n
A typical procedure for synthesis of FJU-POP-1. Under $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere, a mixture of TAPM (110.1 mg, 0.28 mmol), TBPA (178.7 mg, $0.37~\mathrm{mmol}_{.}^{\prime}$ ), ${\mathsf{C u}}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{2}$ $40~\mathrm{mg},0.15~\mathrm{mmol})$ and $\mathsf{K O H}$ ( $140~\mathsf{m g},$ $2.5\:\mathsf{m m o l}$ ) in glycerol $(5~\mathsf{m L})$ was gradually heated to $140^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ and then kept for $72~\mathsf{h}$ . The reaction was cooled down to room temperature and then solvents were removed by filtration. The remaining solids were immersed and centrifuged in acetone and water, then were consecutively activated by $C H_{2}C l_{2}$ and acetone for $24\ h$ and dried at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for another $24\ h$ to obtain the product FJU-POP-1 as brown powders $(70\%,87.3\ m g)$ .
FJU-POP-2 $69\%$ , brown powder), FJU-POP-3 $74\%$ , brown powder) and FJU-POP-4 $(72\%$ , black powder) were prepared using the similar procedure.
